Drag images out and drop data where it is dropped.

Absorbs, in this order: ohos-image-drag-out.patch, ohos-drop-data-at-drop.patch. Their descriptions follow.

--- ohos-image-drag-out.patch

Put a dragged image's bytes where the platform can reach them.

Dragging an image out of a page did nothing on HarmonyOS, and the reason was
two lines above the code that would have handled it: the call that copies the
image's bytes into the OSExchangeData is compiled only for Linux, ChromeOS
and Windows. IS_OHOS is none of those -- it is its own buildflag, with
IS_LINUX at zero -- so PrepareDragForFileContents() never ran, the data
carried no file contents, and by the time the platform was asked to start a
drag there was nothing in it but a URL.

The same file already reads file contents back out on the drop side without
that guard, so this only puts the two directions on the same footing.

What it unlocks: GetSafeFilenameForImageFileContents() names the file from
the page the way a "save image as" would, and keeps the image's own
extension when it is a real image type, so the bytes go out in the format
they arrived in rather than re-encoded.

--- ohos-drop-data-at-drop.patch

Give a page the files that were dropped, not the placeholders from enter.

A .crx dragged onto chrome://extensions did nothing. Two things stood in
the way, one under the other.

HarmonyOS says only which types are coming while the finger is down; the
data itself arrives with the drop. The platform window hands both to the
drop client in turn, and the shell puts a placeholder file in the first so
the page's dragenter sees "Files". But WebContentsViewAura builds its
DropData once, on enter, and at the drop reads the dropped data only if
the target changed under the finger. So the drop event carried the
placeholder: one file called "file", in a directory that does not exist.
chrome://extensions checks the name for .crx, found none, and left it to
the browser, which on this port opens nothing it was not asked to. Any
other page that takes a dropped file had the same problem.

On OHOS the drop now rebuilds DropData from the data that came with it,
keeping the operation already negotiated. Filtering and file grants run
exactly as before: FilterDropData here, the grants in DragTargetDrop.

The second is the extensions page's own. On desktop it stores the dropped
path at dragenter (notifyDragInstallInProgress) and uses it at the drop;
here the path at dragenter is the placeholder. Android has the same shape
-- data only on drop -- and installDroppedFile reads the path at the drop
instead. OHOS does the same. That read comes after the drop is over in
content, when current_drag_data_ is gone, so WebContentsViewAura keeps
the last drop's data for GetDropData() the way Android's view does, until
the next drag enters. GetDropData() has no other caller.

Dragging an extension in is still enabled only in developer mode, as on
desktop.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.
Apply with:

  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-drag-drop.patch

